# strip-client-ip.caddy — SIN-62978 (HIGH) + SIN-62984 (MED) + SIN-62993 (INFO).
#
# Strips every client-supplied client-IP identity header at the edge before the
# request reaches app:8080. Imported by EVERY site block that does
# `reverse_proxy app:8080` — the main Caddyfile, Caddyfile.stg, and the
# (currently dormant) static.Caddyfile.
#
# chi.RealIP (router.go), via the trusted-proxy wrapper in
# internal/adapter/httpapi/trusted_realip.go, reads True-Client-IP → X-Real-IP →
# first token of X-Forwarded-For and rewrites r.RemoteAddr. Without this strip an
# attacker can forge a fresh per-IP bucket key per request and trivially bypass
# the 100/min/IP rate limit on GET /c/{slug} (and any other rate-limit / audit
# path that keys off the trusted client IP). Caddy's reverse_proxy re-adds the
# real client IP in X-Forwarded-For AFTER the strip, so the chi.RealIP read still
# yields the correct peer IP for legitimate traffic.
#
# The Go-side wrapper in trusted_realip.go is belt-and-braces ONLY: it gates
# chi.RealIP on the immediate TCP peer being inside the trusted-proxy CIDR
# allowlist, but Caddy's peer IP lives inside the docker bridge (172.16/12) and
# is therefore trusted by default — i.e. the wrapper relies on Caddy stripping
# forged headers before they arrive.
#
# SIN-62993: extracted from the inline `(strip-client-ip)` snippet in Caddyfile
# into this standalone importable file (same shape as security-headers.caddy) so
# a future app-facing site block that forgets `import strip-client-ip.caddy`
# is the obvious catch in code review. Do NOT inline-duplicate these rules.
request_header -True-Client-IP
request_header -X-Real-IP
request_header -X-Forwarded-For
